(02.14.2026) 
QuestNav (Quest 3S) → Jetson Nano → NT4 → roboRIO (Team 2582, Differential Drive)

What was accomplished
--------------------
1) Standardized a NetworkTables (NT4) “pose stream” contract so the Jetson and roboRIO speak the same language.
2) Implemented robot-side NT4 ingestion (QuestNavIONT4) with health reporting + timestamp conversion.
3) Upgraded QuestNavSubsystem into a real data source (NT4-backed) while keeping its command-friendly API.
4) Infused DriveSubsystem with a DifferentialDrivePoseEstimator scaffold that can fuse Quest pose as a
   “vision-like” measurement (addVisionMeasurement) with safety gates (stale/quality/jump rejection).
5) Verified all method names and key strings match across files, and the project builds successfully.

NT4 Table + Keys (the contract)
------------------------------
All QuestNav pose data is published under this NetworkTables path:

  Table: /questnav

Each key below is a field inside that table.

KEY: x_m
  Type: double
  Units: meters
  Meaning: Field/odometry X position of the robot.
  Notes: In WPILib, X/Y are in meters. X is typically along the field length axis (convention depends on your origin).

KEY: y_m
  Type: double
  Units: meters
  Meaning: Field/odometry Y position of the robot.

KEY: yaw_rad
  Type: double
  Units: radians
  Meaning: Robot heading (Rotation2d) in radians.
  Convention: CCW-positive (WPILib standard).

KEY: ts_s
  Type: double
  Units: seconds (monotonic time on the Jetson)
  Meaning: The timestamp of the pose sample in the Jetson’s own monotonic clock.
  Why it matters: WPILib needs timestamps in the roboRIO timebase for addVisionMeasurement, so
  QuestNavIONT4 estimates an offset:
      rio_time ≈ jetson_time + offset
  and exposes a converted timestamp for the estimator.

KEY: quality
  Type: double
  Range: 0.0 to 1.0 (recommended)
  Meaning: Confidence/quality score for the pose sample.
  Usage: DriveSubsystem rejects pose updates below MIN_QUALITY.

KEY: connected
  Type: boolean
  Meaning: Publisher “alive” flag.
  Usage: If false, robot will not fuse pose samples.

KEY: frame
  Type: string
  Examples: "field", "odom"
  Meaning: Which coordinate frame the pose is expressed in.
  Notes: For FRC pose estimation, field-relative is typically preferred once aligned/reset.

Robot-side behavior (high level)
--------------------------------
- QuestNavIONT4 subscribes to the /questnav keys and keeps the latest sample cached.
- It computes a Jetson→roboRIO time offset so the estimator can use correct timestamps.
- QuestNavSubsystem publishes pose + health to dashboards/Field2d for visualization.
- DriveSubsystem updates its pose estimator each loop and fuses Quest pose when valid:
    - connected must be true
    - sample must not be stale (age <= STALE_TIMEOUT_S)
    - quality must be >= MIN_QUALITY
    - reject teleports (large position jump) and large yaw spikes between samples

Current limitations / next steps (hardware)
-------------------------------------------
- DriveSubsystem currently has stub methods for:
    getHeading()
    getLeftDistanceMeters()
    getRightDistanceMeters()
  Real encoders + gyro are required for true dead-reckoning odometry.
- TRACK_WIDTH_M should be measured/set to match the actual kitbot build.

